WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:00.896
(soft electronic music)
- [Astronaut] Where's

00:00:00.896 --> 00:00:02.100
the direction-
- [Narrator] As a new frontier

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.413
opens in the space market,

00:00:03.413 --> 00:00:07.088
(rocket whooshing)

00:00:07.088 --> 00:00:07.980
(exciting music)

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.950
some companies are trying
to explore the possibilities

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.520
of harvesting resources from
the moon and other objects.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.580
Orbiting the earth is a
potential trillion dollar payload

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.310
of precious minerals and
fuel sources, companies say,

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.080
that could be used to power everyday

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.030
electronic devices and cars.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:24.863
- The moon is

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:25.980
really created from the same materials

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.420
that earth is created from.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.480
- It's about exploration,
to gather those minerals,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.720
and bring it back to earth
in a economically-viable way.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.553
- [Narrator] But as
(soft electronic music)

00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:35.580
investment in space startups

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.070
reaches a record high, some scientists

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.800
are skeptical that lunar
mining could become a reality,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.120
questioning the high cost

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:42.953
of space travel,
(astronaut speaking distantly)

00:00:42.953 --> 00:00:46.440
and the potential to destroy
valuable geological sites.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.600
One company exploring the possibilities

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.990
of extracting the moon's
resources is Astrobotic.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.960
The startup is working with
NASA to develop lunar lander

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.130
and rover projects,
(airy music)

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.480
which Astrobotic says

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.150
could help unlock the moon's
potential in the future.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.230
But those efforts
(soft electronic music)

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.547
face mounting challenges
in the coming years.

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:05.550
(room humming)
- Here we go.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.800
- [Narrator] This is
the business of space.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.970
A series focusing on

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.170
the innovative startups
(soft electronic music)

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.573
capitalizing on the
commercial space industry.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.920
Pittsburgh was nicknamed
"The Steel City" after its

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.570
famed manufacturing industry.
(atmosphere humming)

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.050
But now, technicians at
Astrobotic want it to be reclaimed

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.494
as a space hub.

00:01:23.494 --> 00:01:24.690
(soft mysterious music)

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.990
And that all starts with
Peregrine, the lander set to travel

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.640
to the moon later this year.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.560
- This is the actual flight article,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.030
and we're actually still completing

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.620
the propulsion systems integration

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.030
from the flight article.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.800
- [Narrator] It's part of
NASA's commercial lunar

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.660
payload program.
(airy music)

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.330
- That program is designed to contract

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.210
with companies like ours to,
again, deliver those payloads

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.000
to the surface of the
moon on robotic vehicles.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.833
- [Narrator] It could become

00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:50.400
the first American spacecraft
(soft electronic music)

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.770
to land on the moon since
the last Apollo mission

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.360
more than 50 years ago.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.497
- [Astronaut] It's one small step for man,

00:01:56.497 --> 00:01:59.593
(spacecraft humming)

00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:01.843
one giant leap for mankind.

00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:04.920
- [Narrator] Peregrine
will deliver technologies

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.270
to be tested on the moon.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.900
If successful, they may be
used in NASA's Artemis mission.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.440
That's the space agency's plan
to return humans to the moon,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.380
with a view of potential
inhabitation and construction

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.360
on the lunar surface,
including the building

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.500
of a space camp.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.200
- Where we're at today is
that, we eventually wanna build

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.020
the infrastructure and
capabilities to stay on the moon.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.500
And things like building
habitats are dependent on

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.330
finding where the
resources are in the moon.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.163
- [Narrator] But some
(soft electronic music)

00:02:31.163 --> 00:02:32.370
scientists say that establishing

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.350
permanent bases would involve destroying

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.330
large quantities of the lunar soil,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.540
which is called regolith,
to make way for buildings.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.840
And that process could destroy
clues as to how the moon,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.120
and by extension, the earth, formed.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:45.990
- So, just moving

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.530
all these terrain can eliminate
that record of the impacts

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.300
on the lunar surface that we have.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.450
But also, the upper part of the

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.930
lunar soil is like a record of
what happened in the previous

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.350
millions years from this, and the sun.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.970
- [Narrator] Companies such as Astrobotic,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.460
and government agencies
like NASA, are interested

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.930
in extracting from the regolith

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.160
to try and understand
(soft music)

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.380
the composition of its resources.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.380
Their aim is to facilitate
continued exploration

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.510
and economic development in space.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.030
Scientists have previously
discovered a variety

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.710
of minerals and elements on the moon

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.720
that could be used to support that goal.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.200
- We can find their
oxygen in high quantities

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.770
that will be very helpful
for life support system,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.270
or for propellant and fuel.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.270
We also can find water
ice in the lunar poles,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.960
that also it's very useful
for survival of humans.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.450
We can find other minerals
and other elements

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.853
like silicone, metals,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.370
rare earth elements

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.320
that we can use to fabricate things

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.840
that we need to use there
on the lunar surface.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.950
- [Narrator] But some engineers

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.230
are skeptical of the
possibilities of extracting

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.400
usable material in

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.540
a space environment.
(soft electronic music)

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.090
As many technologies are
still in development.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.370
A lunar mining operation
would have to make use

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.100
of an unprecedented amount
of innovative tools,

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.470
with automated or remote
control machinery,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.170
and pumps that require a
minimal human oversight.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.330
And there are financial
limitations to harvesting

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.670
those resources for use on earth.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.490
The cost of fueling trips to
and from the lunar surface

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.560
is not currently economically viable,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.703
some researchers say.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.010
Payloads traveling
(soft electronic music)

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.090
in the Peregrine lander

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.610
can be bought by private
companies, investors,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.130
or government agencies such as NASA.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.800
The U.S. space agency has
commissioned Astrobotic

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.190
to work on a series of rovers
as payloads, small robots

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.290
that will travel across
the surface of the moon,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.420
and conduct scientific experiments.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.360
One of those being developed
here is the cube rover.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.280
- Cube rovers can be used
for lotsa different missions

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.210
and applications.
(cube rover humming)

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.370
One of the ones that we're
really excited about here,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.450
is that we have rovers that
can drive several kilometers,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.930
survive lunar nights, using
small platforms that are

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.080
orders of magnitude
cheaper than prior rovers

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.030
in the past, allows us to scan the surface

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:02.030
of the moon in a much more
affordable and less complex way,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:03.413
so that we can
(cube rover humming)

00:05:03.413 --> 00:05:05.520
understand where the
resources are located.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.410
There's an interesting
phenomenon that happens

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.560
with smaller rovers that
we're learning more about

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.200
compared to larger rovers,
and that the smaller rovers

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.850
actually float on top of the surface.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.030
So, your wheel designs
for these smaller rovers

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:19.590
are likely going to be very different

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.420
than some of the larger rovers.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.920
- [Narrator] But there
are lots of challenges

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.930
associated with engineering robotics

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.910
to work on the lunar surface.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.520
- The lunar surface have
really extreme temperatures,

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.010
and really high variation of temperatures.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.960
So that is a challenge for the subsystems

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.690
of the rovers and the robotics elements,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.310
but also the terrain.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.310
It has a huge amount of
dust that is really abrasive

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:45.420
and corrosive, and it can
damage the subsystems.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:50.197
Also radiation, the radiation
can play an impact on the,

00:05:50.197 --> 00:05:52.397
especially in the
electronics of the system.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:54.473
- [Narrator] As many
(soft electronic music)

00:05:54.473 --> 00:05:56.460
startups race to capitalize on the moon,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.230
engineers and scientists say there are

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.630
limitations and consequences.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.630
One of them is the potential
damage to the environment.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.870
- There are drawbacks to
going to the moon permanently.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.780
Some examples include
leaving our assets behind

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.530
on the surface, or
disrupting maybe the far side

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.720
of the moon, which is totally
undisturbed right now.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.060
It's looked at as one of the locations

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.280
in our solar system where you
could look deep into space,

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.340
and have no interruptions,
no noise, background noise

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.320
that can influence your observations.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.030
- [Narrator] But given
the environmental costs

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.090
of mining the earth, some
say that the moon's resources

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.800
could help reduce damage at home.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.920
Companies such as Astrobotic
say those potential benefits

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:36.510
outweigh the risk of disrupting

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.130
the moon's natural ecosystem.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.130
- I think that the ability to
harvest resources and use them

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.760
to protect our own planet
is incredibly helpful.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:45.600
- [Narrator] Other scientists

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.590
are more skeptical
(soft electronic music)

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.440
that the moon could, in
turn, help protect the earth.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.500
But one point they can agree
on, is that establishing bases

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.950
on the moon for human visitation
could be decades away.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.130
And harvesting resources
could take even longer.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.500
For now, looking for clues in the regolith

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.613
is the first step.

